"Planning a pregnancy on a GLP-1? Here's what the OB-GYNs' new guidance actually says." A calm walk-through of what ACOG advises clinicians:
- stop 1 to 2 months before pregnancy;
- monitor the baby's weight gain if the medicine is used while breastfeeding;
- why the data are still limited;
- the reassurance in the separate July 2026 international consensus (Obesity Reviews): current data show no increased risk of birth defects after accidental early exposure.
Also cover the tirzepatide and oral-contraceptive interaction as a question to ask your prescriber. Credit everything to ACOG as clinician guidance. Never give viewers stop or timing instructions; say "plan this with your prescriber".

"Protein on Tirzepatide: How Much and Why" (lane A), refreshed with today's headline:
- vitamin D deficiency was the most common abnormality, at 7.5% of users by 6 months and 13.6% by 12 months;
- about 43% of users had low protein intake;
- ferritin (iron stores) ran lower than in people on SGLT2 inhibitors.
Then practical habits: protein first, food-based sources, and asking your clinician about lab checks. Present it as getting more from the medicine, not as something to fear. No supplement dosing.

The 20:00 funnel Short for our best long-form (EloraTZP, 523 views). It asks one fresh question: "What does EloraTZP's next trial test?"
The answer:
- the new trial covers people without diabetes;
- the Phase 2 trial was in type 2 diabetes: 23.3% weight loss on the top combination versus 14.8% on tirzepatide 15 mg at 48 weeks;
- 10.8–27.0% of people on the combination stopped because of side effects, versus 2.9% on tirzepatide.
It ends by naming the live EloraTZP video, using a link checked with oEmbed.

Lane P: "What Is CagriSema?" Explain how cagrilintide (an amylin agonist) pairs with semaglutide. Cover what REDEFINE 1 showed:
- the population: obesity, or overweight with a weight-related condition, without diabetes;
- common GI side effects;
- the dated status: "FDA decision expected Q4 2026, per Novo".
It's ready to republish the day the FDA rules, and can be re-cut as a Lane C video. Don't frame it as "beating" tirzepatide.

Lane A long-form: "What Happens When You Stop Tirzepatide?" Cover:
- the trial evidence on weight regain and on blood-pressure and blood-sugar markers drifting back;
- why so many people stop in real life;
- habits linked to keeping results.
Say plainly, early and at the end: "don't stop or change your medicine on your own; plan it with your prescriber."
